      "message": "[Gerrit AI Review] ✅ **LGTM** — All checks pass.\n\n### Review Summary\n**Functionality \u0026 Security:**\n- The changes successfully eliminate heap buffer overflow vulnerabilities by completely delegating memory allocation and reallocation to Zephyr\u0027s native `k_heap_calloc` and `k_heap_realloc` functions. This safely handles `count * size` multiplications and bounds memory copies during reallocation.\n- `plat_calloc` and `plat_realloc` are cleanly implemented as thin wrappers over `sys_alloc` and `sys_realloc`. Since `k_heap_calloc` natively zero-initializes memory, the redundant `memset` has been safely removed.\n- `sys_free` and `sys_alloc_usable_size` correctly handle `NULL` pointers, making the allocator APIs robust.\n- Expected panic behavior for `sys_alloc` is retained while safe behaviors for `sys_realloc` with `size \u003d\u003d 0` are properly implemented.\n\n**Style \u0026 Consistency:**\n- The code changes are concise, idiomatic, and adhere perfectly to Zephyr\u0027s coding guidelines.\n- The use of `CONFIG_ZTEST` guards to expose internal functions to the test suite without polluting the global namespace is a clean approach.\n\n**Testing:**\n- The `ztest` suite provides rigorous and comprehensive coverage of the new allocator logic.\n- Using `ztest_set_fault_valid(true)` to ensure that intentional zero-sized allocations, integer overflows, and OOM conditions successfully trigger fatal panics (`k_oops()`) is an excellent testing strategy.\n- Memory shrinking and growing behaviors for `realloc` are thoroughly verified.\n\nTAG\u003dagy\nCONV\u003d73c54ef9-4dcc-4b01-8872-acc2584ccc5a",
